<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diyar Rabi'a</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diyar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabia">Rabi'a</a>, 'the abode of the Rabi'a', was the eastern province of the Jazira in upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in early Islamic times, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a> and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>. It took its name from the Arab tribes of the Rabi'a group, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-taghlib">Banu Taghlib</a>, who settled there before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> it was governed from Mosul, and in the 10th century it formed the core of the Hamdanid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Diyar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabia">Rabi'a</a> was the easternmost and largest of the three provinces of the Jazira, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>, the other two being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-bakr">Diyar Bakr</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-mudar">Diyar Mudar</a>. According to the medieval geographer al-Baladhuri, each of the three was named after the main Arab tribe settled in it.</p><p>The province took in the upper reaches of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> and its tributaries, with the regions of Tur Abdin and Beth Arabaye, and both banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> from near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cizre">Jazirat ibn Umar</a> in the north to the boundary with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> near Tikrit in the south, including the lower courses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zab">Upper Zab</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-zab">Lower Zab</a>. Its main city was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, in Arabic al-Mawsil, and the province's other important urban centres were Balad, Jazirat ibn Umar, al-Sinn, Barqa'id, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a> and Ra's al-'Ayn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 690s the region became part of the province of the Jazira, whose oversight was given to Muhammad ibn Marwan, who governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a>. Diyar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabia">Rabi'a</a> was probably not yet a separate administrative subdivision during the Umayyad period and seems to have been created as one only in Abbasid times. During the Abbasid period the region was plagued by the raids of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a>.</p><p>In the mid-10th century the province came under the control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanid dynasty</a>, a family native to the region whose power was centred at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> put an end to the Hamdanid emirate in 980, and Diyar Rabi'a then passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqaylid-dynasty">Uqaylids</a>. They held it until the Seljuq conquest in the late 11th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diyar Rabi'a” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son al-Hasan, given the title Nasir al-Dawla in 942, turned the governorship into a hereditary emirate over Mosul and the <b>Diyar Rabi'a</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamdanid-emirate-of-mosul">Hamdanid emirate of Mosul</a></li></ul></section>
